What plaso (the log2timeline/plaso:20260720 Docker image) reads with its
default Windows Registry and property list plugins, the oracles of
tests/registry_keys.rs (windows.registry.key) and tests/macos_plist.rs
(macos.plist). Made on 2026-10-08 on a Debian host with Docker.

plaso-registry-keys.tsv.gz: every key of the hives tests/fetch-hives.sh
fetches into tests/fixtures/hives/ (Eric Zimmerman's SYSTEM, SOFTWARE,
NTUSER.DAT and ERZ_Win81_UsrClass.dat, MIT; Andrew Rathbun's Windows 10
SYSTEM, SOFTWARE and NTUSER.DAT, MIT; plaso's NTUSER-WIN7.DAT, Apache-2.0)
but Andrew Rathbun's SOFTWARE, whose 246,991 keys would double the file's
size (5.8 MB more, gzipped): they matched too, every one, when this was
made; add them back by leaving out the grep below. One line per key,
sorted: the hive, the key path, its last write (FILETIME), and the values
as plaso's formatter writes them (gen_registry_keys.py says how, and checks
each line against plaso's own message).

  mkdir hives && cp tests/fixtures/hives/* hives/
  sudo docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/data" log2timeline/plaso:20260720 \
    log2timeline --status_view none -q --parsers winreg/winreg_default \
    --storage-file /data/winreg.plaso /data/hives
  sudo docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/data" log2timeline/plaso:20260720 \
    psort --status_view none -q -a -o json_line -w /data/winreg.jsonl /data/winreg.plaso
  python3 -I gen_registry_keys.py winreg.jsonl \
    | grep -v '^rathbun-win10-SOFTWARE' | gzip -9n > plaso-registry-keys.tsv.gz

plaso-registry-edges.tsv: the keys of tests/fixtures/registry-edges/
NTUSER-edges.DAT (plaso's NTUSER-WIN7.DAT with eleven values changed by
patch.py, see its NOTICE) holding the changed values, the same way:

  mkdir edges && gzip -dc NTUSER-edges.DAT.gz > edges/NTUSER-edges.DAT
  (log2timeline and psort as above, on /data/edges, into edges.jsonl)
  python3 -I gen_registry_keys.py edges.jsonl | grep -E \
    '	HKEY_CURRENT_USER.(Control Panel.Appearance.New Schemes.0.Sizes.0|Control Panel.Desktop|Software.Microsoft.Cryptography.CertificateTemplateCache.Administrator)	' \
    > plaso-registry-edges.tsv

plaso-plist-keys.tsv: every event of the 27 files of plaso's
test_data/plist/ at commit 91b6849503b125267d00ea134248e4fe59e5479c
(tests/fixtures/plist-default/, Apache-2.0, see its NOTICE); one line per
event, sorted: the file, the root, the key, the date in microseconds.

  mkdir plist && cp tests/fixtures/plist-default/*.plist plist/
  sudo docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/data" log2timeline/plaso:20260720 \
    log2timeline --status_view none -q --parsers plist/plist_default \
    --storage-file /data/plist.plaso /data/plist
  sudo docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/data" log2timeline/plaso:20260720 \
    psort --status_view none -q -a -o json_line -w /data/plist.jsonl /data/plist.plaso
  python3 -I gen_plist_keys.py plist.jsonl > plaso-plist-keys.tsv

(psort's -a keeps events it would drop as duplicates. With only the
default plugin named, plaso's named plist plugins are off and its default
one reads every file; the default Registry plugin reads every key.)

What the comparison found:

- Registry: all 494,349 keys of the eight fetched hives (247,358 of them in
  the committed oracle) at the same path, with the same last write and the
  same values line. Learnt from it, and done as plaso does: numbers are
  signed (a REG_DWORD 0xFFFFFFFF is -1), read from the leading 4 or 8 bytes
  of longer data (a REG_DWORD of 8 bytes is its first 4); a REG_MULTI_SZ
  holds only the strings a NUL ends, up to the first empty one (a list
  without a final NUL loses its last string, one without any NUL is []).
- Registry, NTUSER-edges.DAT: as plaso, a REG_SZ of an odd size, a REG_DWORD
  or REG_QWORD shorter than its type are "(n bytes)"; an odd last byte of a
  REG_MULTI_SZ is ignored; a leading byte order mark is dropped; REG_LINK is
  its text's length in characters, written "(n bytes)"; REG_DWORD_BE is read
  big-endian, signed. Two differences, on purpose, which the test spells
  out: an unpaired UTF-16 surrogate, which Python keeps, is U+FFFD (a Rust
  string can't hold one); a name stored one byte per character is read as
  Latin-1, as Windows writes it (its UTF-16 units truncated), where plaso
  reads Windows-1252 (bytes 0x80 to 0x9F differ: 0x92 is U+0092 here,
  U+2019 there).
- Registry, not seen in any hive and done differently: plaso fails a hive
  that fits two mappings of different roots (a RuntimeError in dfwinreg)
  where this writes its keys rooted at "\"; and a key whose values plaso's
  libregf can't read is lost in plaso, where this keeps the key, the values
  it could read, and reports the others as skipped.
- Property lists: all 66 events of plaso in the 27 files, same roots, keys
  and dates to the microsecond. And three keys more: plaso (Python's
  plistlib) refuses com.apple.security.KCN.plist for its date in the year 0
  and loses the whole file; read as CoreFoundation reads it, its three
  dates are records. empty.plist (no value) and truncated.plist (a binary
  trailer pointing outside the file) give nothing, in plaso too. Not in the
  test data and done differently: a dictionary repeating a key keeps the
  last value (as plistlib) at the last place (plistlib, the first); a file
  partly damaged gives what can be read, with the damage reported, where
  plistlib gives up on it.
